## 1. PROJECT STRUCTURE OVERVIEW

```
JobPortal/
├── Program.cs                     ← Entry point
├── Core/
│   ├── Interfaces/                ← Contracts (IUserProfile, IJobService, INotifiable)
│   ├── Enums/                     ← ApplicationStatus, JobStatus, InterviewStatus, ReportType
│   ├── DTOs/                      ← Data Transfer Objects (thin read-only bags)
│   ├── Structs/                   ← SalaryRange (value type)
│   └── Exceptions/                ← Custom exception hierarchy
├── Features/
│   ├── Auth/                      ← AuthService (static), OTPService
│   ├── Candidate/                 ← BaseUser (abstract), JobSeeker, ResumeService
│   ├── Admin/                     ← Admin (inherits BaseUser)
│   ├── JobPosting/                ← JobListing, JobSearch, filters, JobConfig
│   ├── Application/               ← Application (with event)
│   ├── Interview/                 ← Interview, InterviewScheduler
│   ├── Notifications/             ← Notification, NotificationService, delegate
│   └── Reports/                   ← ReportManager (static)
├── FileStorage/
│   ├── FileStorageService.cs      ← Generic JSON persistence
│   └── DataStore.cs               ← Central static data access layer
└── UI/
    └── ConsoleHelper.cs           ← Console formatting utilities
```

The project deliberately separates **Core** (pure domain rules, no I/O) from **Features** (business logic) and **FileStorage** (persistence). This is a simplified **Layered Architecture**.

---

## 2. NAMESPACE

### Where it appears
Every single file begins with a `namespace` declaration, e.g.:
```csharp
namespace JobPortal.Core.Exceptions;
namespace JobPortal.Features.Candidate;
namespace JobPortal.FileStorage;
```

### What it is
A namespace is a logical container that groups related types. It prevents name conflicts — two classes can both be named `Application` if they live in different namespaces.

### Why used HERE
- `JobPortal.Core.Exceptions` groups all custom exceptions together so you can `catch (JPNSException)` to catch any portal-specific error.
- `JobPortal.Features.Candidate` keeps `JobSeeker` and `BaseUser` together since they are part of the same feature.
- The folder structure mirrors the namespace, making navigation predictable.

### Why you CANNOT skip it
Without namespaces, `Application` (the job application) and `Application` (the .NET application process) would clash. The compiler would be unable to resolve which one you mean.

---

## 3. ACCESS MODIFIERS

### Where they appear
Almost every declaration uses one: `internal class`, `private string _name`, `protected string _id`, `public string Name =>`.

### Definitions

| Modifier | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `public` | Accessible from anywhere |
| `internal` | Accessible only within this assembly (this project) |
| `protected` | Accessible only within the class and its subclasses |
| `private` | Accessible only within the same class |

### Why `internal` for classes
Every class (`JobSeeker`, `Admin`, `JobListing`, etc.) is `internal`. This is correct because this is a single-assembly console application — no external library needs to use these classes. If you made them `public`, you'd be promising an API contract to the outside world unnecessarily.

### Why `private` for fields
```csharp
// In JobSeeker.cs
private string _name;
private string _email;
private string _passwordHash;
```
The fields are `private` so external code cannot directly set `_passwordHash = "abc"`. It must go through `UpdatePassword()`, which runs `ValidatePassword()` first. This is **encapsulation** (see section 12).

### Why `protected` in BaseUser
```csharp
// In BaseUser.cs
protected string _id;
protected string _email;
protected string _passwordHash;
```
`JobSeeker` and `Admin` both inherit from `BaseUser`. They need to write to `_id` and `_email` during their own constructors. `protected` allows this. If they were `private`, child classes could never initialize them. If they were `public`, any code anywhere could change the email or ID without validation.

### Why NOT use `public` fields everywhere
If `_passwordHash` were `public string PasswordHash;` (a field, not a property), you could do `seeker.PasswordHash = "abc"` from anywhere with no hashing. The entire security model collapses.

---

## 4. ABSTRACT CLASS – BaseUser

### File: `Features/Candidate/BaseUser.cs`

```csharp
internal abstract class BaseUser
{
    protected string _id;
    protected string _email;
    protected string _passwordHash;
    protected bool   _isActive;
    protected DateTime _createdAt;
    protected DateTime _lastLoginAt;

    public virtual void Login()  { _lastLoginAt = DateTime.Now; }
    public virtual void Logout() { }
    public abstract string GetRole();
    public void UpdateLastLogin() => _lastLoginAt = DateTime.Now;
}
```

### What is an abstract class
An abstract class is a class that **cannot be instantiated directly**. It exists only to be inherited. It can contain:
- Concrete (implemented) methods — shared behaviour
- Abstract methods — methods that subclasses MUST implement
- Fields and properties — shared state

### Why `abstract` HERE
`BaseUser` holds fields shared by both `JobSeeker` and `Admin`: `_id`, `_email`, `_passwordHash`, `_isActive`, `_createdAt`, `_lastLoginAt`. Without it, both classes would duplicate these six fields and the `UpdateLastLogin()` logic. That's copy-paste code — a maintenance nightmare.

A raw `BaseUser` object has no meaning in this system. There is no such thing as a "user who is neither a seeker nor an admin". Making it `abstract` enforces this. The compiler will refuse `new BaseUser()`.

### Why NOT a regular (non-abstract) class
If `BaseUser` were not `abstract`, someone could write `new BaseUser()` and get an object with no role, no seeker ID, no admin ID — a half-baked entity that would break every feature. Abstract prevents this.

### Why NOT an interface
An interface cannot hold fields or constructors. `BaseUser` needs to hold `_email`, `_passwordHash`, and initialize them in a constructor. An interface cannot do that.

---

## 5. INHERITANCE

### Where it appears

```csharp
// JobSeeker inherits BaseUser
internal class JobSeeker : BaseUser, IUserProfile, INotifiable { ... }

// Admin inherits BaseUser
internal class Admin : BaseUser { ... }

// SkillsFilter and SalaryFilter inherit BaseJobFilter
internal class SkillsFilter : BaseJobFilter { ... }
internal class SalaryFilter : BaseJobFilter { ... }

// All custom exceptions inherit JPNSException
internal class ValidationException : JPNSException { ... }
internal class AccountLockedException : JPNSException { ... }
```

### What is inheritance
Inheritance means a child class automatically receives all non-private members (fields, methods, properties) of its parent class. The child can add new members or override parent methods.

### Why used for JobSeeker / Admin
Both `JobSeeker` and `Admin` share: `_id`, `_email`, `_passwordHash`, `_isActive`, `_createdAt`, `_lastLoginAt`, `Login()`, `Logout()`, `UpdateLastLogin()`. Inheritance lets both classes get all of that for free. Only role-specific things are added:
- `JobSeeker` adds: `_seekerId`, `_skills`, `_resumeUrl`, `SavedJobs`, `Verify()`, etc.
- `Admin` adds: `_adminId`, `_company`, `_isApproved`, `AddJobListing()`, etc.

### Why inheritance for filters
`SkillsFilter` and `SalaryFilter` both need `FilterJobs()` and `CalculateRelevance()`. `BaseJobFilter` declares these as `abstract`, forcing both to implement them. The shared `GetFilterSummary()` method is inherited for free.

### Why inheritance for exceptions
```csharp
internal class JPNSException : Exception { ... }
internal class ValidationException : JPNSException { ... }
internal class AccountLockedException : JPNSException { ... }
```
This hierarchy means a `catch (JPNSException)` block in Program.cs catches ALL portal-specific errors in one go. You can also catch specific ones (like `AccountLockedException`) before the general one. This is called **exception polymorphism**.

### Why NOT composition instead of inheritance here
For `BaseUser`, composition would mean each class holds a `UserInfo` object. That's valid too, but then `Admin` and `JobSeeker` would need to forward calls like `admin.userInfo.Email` instead of just `admin.Email`. Inheritance is simpler when the relationship is clearly "IS-A" (Admin IS-A BaseUser).

---

## 6. INTERFACES

### Files: `Core/Interfaces/IUserProfile.cs`, `IJobService.cs`, `INotifiable.cs`

```csharp
internal interface IUserProfile
{
    void UpdateProfile();
    void Display();
    string GetProfileSummary();
}

internal interface IJobService
{
    List<JobListing> GetJobList();
    void ApplyForJob(string jobId);
}

internal interface INotifiable
{
    void SendNotification(string message);
}
```

### What is an interface
An interface is a **pure contract** — it lists method signatures with no implementation, no fields. Any class that implements the interface **must** provide all those methods.

### Why `IUserProfile` on JobSeeker
`JobSeeker` must implement `UpdateProfile()`, `Display()`, and `GetProfileSummary()`. The interface ensures that if tomorrow you add a `FreelancerProfile` class, it also has the same methods. You could write code like:
```csharp
IUserProfile profile = seeker;
profile.Display(); // works for any user type
```

### Why `INotifiable` on JobSeeker AND Application
Both `JobSeeker` and `Application` implement `INotifiable`. This means anything that can be notified has a `SendNotification()` method. Code that needs to send a notification can just hold an `INotifiable` reference — it doesn't need to know if it's a seeker or an application.

### Why NOT abstract class instead of interfaces
A class can only inherit **one** abstract class, but can implement **multiple** interfaces.  
`JobSeeker : BaseUser, IUserProfile, INotifiable` — it already inherits from `BaseUser`. It cannot also inherit from a second abstract class. Interfaces solve this. If `IUserProfile` were an abstract class, `JobSeeker` couldn't use it because it already extends `BaseUser`.

---

## 7. MULTIPLE INTERFACE IMPLEMENTATION

### Where it appears
```csharp
// JobSeeker.cs
internal class JobSeeker : BaseUser, IUserProfile, INotifiable
```

### What it means
`JobSeeker` promises to implement ALL methods from both `IUserProfile` (UpdateProfile, Display, GetProfileSummary) AND `INotifiable` (SendNotification), PLUS inherit everything from `BaseUser`.

### Why used here
A job seeker has a profile (can be displayed, updated, summarised) AND can receive notifications. These are two different capabilities. Separating them into two interfaces keeps each interface small and focused — this is the **Interface Segregation Principle**.

### Why NOT one big interface
If there was one `IMegaUser` interface with all six methods, then classes that only need notification (like `Application`) would be forced to implement profile methods that make no sense for them. Keeping interfaces small prevents bloated, meaningless implementations.

---

## 8. SEALED CLASS – JobConfig

### File: `Features/JobPosting/JobConfig.cs`

```csharp
internal sealed class JobConfig
{
    public const int MaxDescriptionLength = 2000;
    public const int MinSkillCount        = 1;
    public const int MaxSavedJobs         = 50;
    public const int MaxTitleLength       = 200;
    public const int MinTitleLength       = 3;
    public const int MaxCoverLetterLength = 1000;
    public const int MaxExpiryDays        = 365;
}
```

### What `sealed` means
`sealed` prevents any class from inheriting from `JobConfig`. No one can do `class MyConfig : JobConfig`.

### Why sealed here
`JobConfig` is a pure configuration bag — just constants. It should never be overridden or extended. Sealing it communicates clearly: "this class is final; the values are fixed portal-wide rules."

### Why `const` and not regular fields
`const` values are baked in at compile time. They cost zero memory at runtime. Regular `int` fields would allocate an object instance. Since these are never changing, `const` is the right choice. 

### Why NOT static class
A static class could also hold constants. But `sealed` class is chosen here — it still allows the class to be instantiated if needed (e.g., for JSON serialization in the future), while still preventing inheritance.

---

## 9. STATIC CLASS AND STATIC MEMBERS

### Where it appears

```csharp
// AuthService.cs — entire class is static
internal static class AuthService { ... }

// ReportManager.cs — entire class is static
internal static class ReportManager { ... }

// DataStore.cs — entire class is static
internal static class DataStore { ... }

// JobSearch.cs — all methods are static
internal class JobSearch
{
    public static List<JobListing> SearchJobs(...) { ... }
}

// JobListing.cs — static property
public static int TotalPostings { get; private set; } = 0;
```

### What `static` means on a CLASS
A static class cannot be instantiated — `new AuthService()` is a compiler error. All its members must also be static. It acts like a collection of utility functions.

### Why `AuthService` is static
Authentication logic doesn't need instance state. `HashPassword()`, `VerifyPassword()`, `ValidateEmail()` are pure functions — they take inputs and return outputs without depending on any stored object state. Making them static means you call them directly: `AuthService.HashPassword(password)` from anywhere without creating an AuthService object.

### Why `DataStore` is static
`DataStore` is the single source of truth for all data in the application. There should be exactly one — not one per user or one per menu. Static enforces this singleton-like behavior. All code accesses the same `DataStore.Jobs`, `DataStore.JobSeekers`, etc.

### Why `TotalPostings` is a static PROPERTY on JobListing
It tracks how many `JobListing` objects have been created across the entire application. This is object-level metadata, not instance metadata. Each instance can't know about all other instances — only a static (class-level) counter can do that.

### Why NOT make everything static
`JobSeeker`, `Admin`, `JobListing`, `Application` all need per-object state. Each seeker has their own name, email, and skills. Making these static would mean there's only one global name — obviously wrong. Static is for shared, stateless utilities or class-level data.

---

## 10. READONLY FIELDS AND CONST

### Where they appear

```csharp
// AuthService.cs
private const int LockDurationMinutes = 30;
private const int MaxFailedAttempts   = 5;
private static readonly Dictionary<string, DateTime> _lockTimestamps = new();

// ResumeService.cs
private static readonly HashSet<string> _allowedExtensions = new(...);
private const long MaxFileSizeBytes = 5 * 1024 * 1024;

// FileStorageService.cs
private static readonly JsonSerializerOptions _opts = new() { ... };
```

### Difference between `const` and `readonly`

| | `const` | `readonly` |
|---|---|---|
| Set when | Compile time | At declaration or in constructor |
| Can be object | No (primitives/strings only) | Yes |
| Static by default | Yes | No (must add `static` explicitly) |

### Why `const` for numbers (MaxFailedAttempts = 5)
These are fixed integers known at compile time. `const` is more efficient (zero runtime allocation) and signals "this will never change".

### Why `readonly` for `_lockTimestamps`
A `Dictionary` is an object — `const` can't hold objects. `readonly` means `_lockTimestamps` always points to the same Dictionary instance (you can't reassign it), but you can still add/remove entries. This is the correct way to make a shared collection that is initialized once.

### Why `readonly` for `_opts` (JsonSerializerOptions)
`JsonSerializerOptions` is expensive to construct (it builds internal lookup tables). Creating it once and reusing it with `readonly static` is a performance best practice recommended by Microsoft.

### Why NOT use regular fields
If `MaxFailedAttempts` were a regular field, anyone could accidentally write `MaxFailedAttempts = 100;` somewhere. `const` prevents reassignment entirely, making the intent clear.

---

## 11. PROPERTIES WITH EXPRESSION-BODY GETTERS

### Where they appear

```csharp
// BaseUser.cs
public string   Id           => _id;
public string   Email        => _email;
public string   PasswordHash => _passwordHash;
public bool     IsActive     => _isActive;

// JobSeeker.cs
public string SeekerId   => _seekerId;
public string Name       => _name;
public bool   IsVerified => _isVerified;
```

### What they are
`public string Name => _name;` is shorthand for:
```csharp
public string Name { get { return _name; } }
```
It is a **read-only property** — you can read it from outside, but only the class itself can write to `_name`.

### Why used throughout
This is the backbone of encapsulation. External code can read `seeker.Name` to display it, but cannot do `seeker.Name = "hacked"`. Changes go through validated methods like `seeker.UpdateName("Alice")`, which calls `ValidateName()` first.

### Why expression-body (`=>`) instead of full `{ get; set; }`
`{ get; set; }` would expose a setter, allowing unvalidated writes. The `=>` syntax creates a getter-only shortcut that's clean and concise for simple "return the field" properties.

---

## 12. ENCAPSULATION

### The pattern used everywhere
```csharp
// Private field — only the class can write to it
private string _name;

// Public property — anyone can read it
public string Name => _name;

// Public method — validates before changing the field
public void UpdateName(string name)
{
    Auth.AuthService.ValidateName(name);
    _name = name.Trim();
}
```

### What encapsulation means
Encapsulation means bundling data (fields) and the code that operates on them (methods) together, while hiding internal details and controlling access through a defined interface.

### Why this pattern is correct
1. `_name` is private → no one can set it to empty/null from outside.
2. `Name` property is read-only → anyone can see the name.
3. `UpdateName()` validates before writing → the field is always in a valid state.

Without this pattern, you could do `seeker._passwordHash = "abc"` from anywhere and bypass the BCrypt hashing. The entire security model depends on encapsulation.

### Why NOT use auto-properties with public setters everywhere
```csharp
// BAD — exposes setter to everyone
public string PasswordHash { get; set; }
```
This lets any code (including UI code) directly write a raw password string into `PasswordHash`. There would be no enforced validation or hashing.

---

## 13. CONSTRUCTOR OVERLOADING

### Where it appears — JobSeeker.cs
```csharp
// Overload 1: Parameterless (for JSON deserialization)
public JobSeeker() : base() { ... }

// Overload 2: Full registration constructor
public JobSeeker(string name, string email, string phone, string password) : base() { ... }
```

### Also in Application.cs
```csharp
public Application() { ... }                          // for deserialization
public Application(string jobSeekerId, string jobId, string resumeSnapshot, ...) { ... }  // normal creation
public Application(Application existing) { ... }      // copy constructor
```

### What constructor overloading means
Multiple constructors with different parameter lists. The compiler chooses the right one based on what arguments you pass.

### Why the PARAMETERLESS constructor
`System.Text.Json` deserializes JSON by first calling the parameterless constructor, then setting properties. Without `JobSeeker()`, the application crashes on startup when loading saved data from `jobseekers.json`. This constructor exists **purely for the serializer**.

### Why the FULL constructor validates immediately
```csharp
public JobSeeker(string name, string email, string phone, string password) : base()
{
    Auth.AuthService.ValidateName(name);    // ← throws if invalid
    Auth.AuthService.ValidateEmail(email);  // ← throws if invalid
    ...
}
```
By validating in the constructor, it is **impossible to create a JobSeeker with an invalid email**. The object either exists in a valid state or doesn't exist at all. This is called **fail-fast construction**.

### Why `: base()` after the constructor signature
`JobSeeker` inherits from `BaseUser`. `: base()` calls `BaseUser`'s parameterless constructor, which initializes the inherited fields (`_isActive = true`, `_createdAt = DateTime.Now`, etc.). Without this, those fields would be uninitialized.

---

## 14. VIRTUAL AND OVERRIDE METHODS

### Where they appear

```csharp
// BaseUser.cs — declared as virtual (can be overridden)
public virtual void Login()  { _lastLoginAt = DateTime.Now; }
public virtual void Logout() { }

// JobListing.cs — virtual method that subclasses can override
public virtual void ExportToFile(string path) { File.WriteAllText(path, ToString()); }
```

### What `virtual` means
A `virtual` method provides a default implementation that subclasses **may** override using the `override` keyword. If they don't override it, the base implementation is used.

### Why `Login()` is virtual
`BaseUser.Login()` updates `_lastLoginAt`. That's the common behavior. But if a future `PremiumSeeker` class needed to also log the login IP or send an analytics event, it could override `Login()`:
```csharp
public override void Login()
{
    base.Login();        // still updates _lastLoginAt
    LogAnalytics();      // adds premium-specific behavior
}
```

### Why NOT make Login abstract
`abstract` means the base class provides NO implementation. But `Login()` has a perfectly good default (`_lastLoginAt = DateTime.Now`). Both `JobSeeker` and `Admin` use that default, so there's no reason to force them to re-implement it. `virtual` is the right choice when there IS a sensible default.

---

## 15. ABSTRACT METHOD – GetRole

### Where it appears

```csharp
// BaseUser.cs
public abstract string GetRole();

// JobSeeker.cs — must implement
public override string GetRole() => "JobSeeker";

// Admin.cs — must implement
public override string GetRole() => "Admin";
```

### What abstract method means
An `abstract` method has NO implementation in the base class. Every non-abstract subclass MUST provide an implementation or the code won't compile.

### Why `GetRole()` is abstract
There is no sensible default role for `BaseUser` — a `BaseUser` has no role. Making it `abstract` guarantees the compiler enforces that every concrete user type (JobSeeker, Admin) defines its own role. You can never accidentally have a user with an undefined role.

### Why NOT use a virtual method returning ""
```csharp
// BAD — compiles even if subclass forgets to override
public virtual string GetRole() => "";
```
If a new `Recruiter` class forgets to override `GetRole()`, it returns `""`. This would silently cause authorization bugs. `abstract` makes it a compile-time error to forget.

---

## 16. ENUM

### Files: `Core/Enums/ApplicationStatus.cs`, `JobStatus.cs`, `InterviewStatus.cs`, `ReportType.cs`

```csharp
internal enum ApplicationStatus
{
    PENDING,
    SHORTLISTED,
    INTERVIEW_SCHEDULED,
    REJECTED,
    HIRED
}

internal enum JobStatus
{
    PENDING_APPROVAL,
    APPROVED,
    REJECTED,
    CLOSED,
    EXPIRED
}
```

### What an enum is
An `enum` defines a named set of integer constants. Instead of `int status = 3`, you write `ApplicationStatus.REJECTED`, which is human-readable and compile-time safe.

### Why enums for status fields
1. **Compile-time safety**: `status = "HIRED_TYPO"` is a compile error. `status = ApplicationStatus.HIRED_TYPO` is also a compile error.
2. **Exhaustive switch**: The compiler warns if a switch expression doesn't handle all enum values.
3. **Readable**: `if (status == ApplicationStatus.SHORTLISTED)` is self-documenting. `if (status == 2)` is mysterious.

### Used in state machine (Application.UpdateStatus)
```csharp
bool valid = (_status, parsed) switch
{
    (ApplicationStatus.PENDING, ApplicationStatus.SHORTLISTED) => true,
    ...
};
```
The switch expression only works cleanly because the statuses are enums. With strings you'd need `.ToUpper()` comparisons everywhere and you'd have no compile-time check for typos.

### Why NOT use string constants
```csharp
// BAD
private const string STATUS_PENDING = "PENDING";
```
Strings can be mistyped silently. Enums are checked by the compiler.

---

## 17. STRUCT – SalaryRange

### File: `Core/Structs/SalaryRange.cs`

```csharp
internal struct SalaryRange
{
    public decimal Min { get; set; }
    public decimal Max { get; set; }

    public SalaryRange(decimal min, decimal max)
    {
        if (min <= 0) throw new ArgumentException("Minimum salary must be greater than 0.");
        if (max < min) throw new ArgumentException("Maximum salary must be >= minimum salary.");
        Min = min;
        Max = max;
    }

    public bool IsInRange(decimal value) => value >= Min && value <= Max;
    public override string ToString() => $"{Min:F0} - {Max:F0} LPA";
}
```

### What a struct is
A struct is a **value type** (like `int`, `double`). When you assign it or pass it to a method, a complete copy is made. It lives on the stack (or inline in the containing object), not on the heap.

### Why struct for SalaryRange
- `SalaryRange` is a small, simple pair of two numbers (`Min` and `Max`).
- It has no identity — two `SalaryRange(5, 10)` instances are equivalent. You don't distinguish between "which salary range object" the way you distinguish between "which JobSeeker".
- Being a value type means no heap allocation and no garbage collection overhead.
- The built-in equality is value-based (two structs are equal if their fields are equal), which is exactly what you want for salary comparisons.

### Why NOT a class for SalaryRange
If `SalaryRange` were a class, each `JobListing` would hold a reference to a heap object. Copying a `JobListing` would share the same `SalaryRange` object — modifying one would accidentally affect the other. Structs copy-by-value, so each `JobListing` always has its own `SalaryRange`.

### Why `decimal` instead of `double`
`decimal` is precise for financial/monetary values. `double` has floating-point rounding errors — `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.30000000000000004` in double. Salary must be exact.

---

## 18. DTO CLASSES

### Files: `Core/DTOs/ApplicationDTO.cs`, `JobSummaryDTO.cs`, `ReportDTO.cs`

```csharp
internal class ApplicationDTO
{
    public string ApplicationId { get; set; } = "";
    public string JobTitle      { get; set; } = "";
    public string Company       { get; set; } = "";
    public string Status        { get; set; } = "";
    public DateTime AppliedDate { get; set; }
    public string CandidateName { get; set; } = "";
    public string CandidateEmail{ get; set; } = "";
}
```

### What a DTO is
A **Data Transfer Object** is a plain class with only public properties and no business logic. It's used to carry data between layers (e.g., from the domain model to the UI or to a report) without exposing the full domain object.

### Why ApplicationDTO exists
`Application` (the domain object) holds `_applicationId`, `_jobSeekerId`, `_jobId`, `_status`, etc. But to display a table to the user, the admin needs to see the job title and candidate name — which are not stored IN the `Application` object (it only has IDs). A DTO is assembled from multiple objects to produce a flat, ready-to-display package.

### Why NOT pass the full `Application` object to the UI
The domain `Application` object contains `OnStatusChanged` event, business methods like `UpdateStatus()`, and can trigger state transitions. Passing it raw to a display layer risks accidental mutation. A DTO is read-only data — safe to pass anywhere.

### Why `{ get; set; } = ""` initialization
The `= ""` sets a default value, ensuring the string is never null. This avoids `NullReferenceException` when displaying before all fields are populated.

---

## 19. CUSTOM EXCEPTION HIERARCHY

### Files: `Core/Exceptions/`

```csharp
// Root of all portal exceptions
internal class JPNSException : Exception
{
    public JPNSException(string message) : base(message) { }
}

// Specific exceptions
internal class ValidationException : JPNSException
{
    public string FieldName { get; }
    public ValidationException(string fieldName, string message)
        : base($"[{fieldName}] {message}") { FieldName = fieldName; }
}

internal class AccountLockedException : JPNSException
{
    public DateTime UnlockAt { get; }
    public AccountLockedException(DateTime unlockAt)
        : base($"Account locked. Try again after {unlockAt:HH:mm:ss}.") { UnlockAt = unlockAt; }
}
```

### What this hierarchy achieves
- `JPNSException` is the base for ALL portal-specific errors
- `ValidationException` carries which field failed validation
- `AccountLockedException` carries WHEN the account will unlock
- `InvalidCredentialException` carries how many attempts remain

### Why NOT just throw `Exception`
```csharp
// BAD — caller can't distinguish a locked account from a server crash
throw new Exception("Account locked");
```
With `throw new AccountLockedException(unlockAt)`, the caller can:
```csharp
catch (AccountLockedException ex)
{
    ShowCountdown(ex.UnlockAt); // use the structured data
}
```
A plain `Exception` is just a message string — no structured data, no type-based routing.

### Why `JPNSException` as a common root
In `Program.cs`:
```csharp
catch (AccountLockedException ex) { ... }  // specific first
catch (InvalidCredentialException ex) { ... }  // specific first
catch (JPNSException ex) { ... }            // catches all remaining portal errors
catch (Exception) { ... }                   // catches unexpected errors
```
The hierarchy allows you to catch broadly OR specifically, depending on what you need at that call site.

---

## 20. TRY-CATCH-FINALLY AND EXCEPTION PROPAGATION

### Where it appears — Program.cs HandleLogin()

```csharp
private static void HandleLogin()
{
    try
    {
        // ... login logic ...
    }
    catch (AccountLockedException ex)
    {
        ConsoleHelper.PrintError(ex.Message);
        ConsoleHelper.Pause();
    }
    catch (InvalidCredentialException ex)
    {
        ConsoleHelper.PrintError(ex.Message);
        ConsoleHelper.Pause();
    }
    catch (JPNSException ex)
    {
        ConsoleHelper.PrintError(ex.Message);
        ConsoleHelper.Pause();
    }
    catch (Exception)
    {
        ConsoleHelper.PrintError("Unexpected error during login.");
        ConsoleHelper.Pause();
    }
}
```

### Why multiple catch blocks in ORDER
C# evaluates catch blocks top to bottom and uses the FIRST matching one. `AccountLockedException` must come BEFORE `JPNSException` because `AccountLockedException` IS a `JPNSException` (inheritance). If `JPNSException` came first, it would always match and `AccountLockedException` would never be reached.

### Why a generic `catch (Exception)` last
This catches unexpected errors (null reference, file I/O errors, etc.) that weren't anticipated. Without it, an unhandled exception would crash the entire application. The program instead shows a friendly error and continues running.

### Why NOT catch everything in one broad `catch (Exception)`
Then you'd lose the ability to show specific messages. "Account locked. Try again after 15:30:00" is far more helpful than "Something went wrong".

### Exception PROPAGATION in TryLoginAsSeeker
```csharp
private static JobSeeker? TryLoginAsSeeker(string identifier, string password)
{
    try   { return AuthService.LoginJobSeeker(identifier, password); }
    catch (AccountLockedException) { throw; }  // ← re-throw this one
    catch  { return null; }                     // ← swallow all others
}
```
`AccountLockedException` is intentionally re-thrown because the caller (HandleLogin) needs to know the account is locked. Other exceptions (wrong password → `InvalidCredentialException`) are swallowed here and return `null` — meaning "not this user".

---

## 21. GENERICS – FileStorageService<T>

### File: `FileStorage/FileStorageService.cs`

```csharp
internal class FileStorageService<T>
{
    private readonly string _filePath;

    public void SaveData(List<T> data) { ... }
    public List<T> LoadData() { ... }
}
```

### Usage
```csharp
// In DataStore.cs
private static FileStorageService<JobSeeker>   _seekerStorage = null!;
private static FileStorageService<Admin>       _adminStorage  = null!;
private static FileStorageService<JobListing>  _jobStorage    = null!;
private static FileStorageService<Application> _appStorage    = null!;
```

### What generics are
`<T>` is a **type parameter** — a placeholder replaced by a real type when you use the class. `FileStorageService<JobSeeker>` becomes a service that saves/loads `List<JobSeeker>`.

### Why one generic class instead of four separate classes
Without generics, you'd need:
```csharp
class JobSeekerStorageService { public List<JobSeeker> LoadData() { ... } }
class AdminStorageService { public List<Admin> LoadData() { ... } }
class JobListingStorageService { public List<JobListing> LoadData() { ... } }
```
All four would be IDENTICAL except for the type name. That's code duplication. The generic version writes the logic once, and the compiler generates the four versions automatically.

### Type safety: why NOT `object` or `dynamic`
```csharp
// BAD — no type safety
public List<object> LoadData() { ... }
```
This would let you mix seekers and jobs in one list. With `<T>`, the type is fixed at compile time — `FileStorageService<JobSeeker>.LoadData()` can only return `List<JobSeeker>`.

---

## 22. DELEGATE AND EVENT

### File: `Features/Notifications/NotificationService.cs` and `Application.cs`

```csharp
// Delegate definition — the "type" of a notification handler
internal delegate void NotifyHandler(string message);

// In Application.cs — an event of that delegate type
public event NotifyHandler? OnStatusChanged;

// Firing the event
OnStatusChanged?.Invoke(msg);
```

### What a delegate is
A delegate is a **type-safe function pointer**. `NotifyHandler` is a type that represents any `void` method taking one `string` parameter. You can store a method in a `NotifyHandler` variable and call it later.

### What an event is
An `event` is a restricted delegate — external code can only subscribe (`+=`) or unsubscribe (`-=`). It cannot invoke the event directly. Only the class that owns it can fire it.

### Why this is used in Application
When an application's status changes (`PENDING → SHORTLISTED`), subscribers need to know. Instead of `Application` directly calling `NotificationService.Send()` (which would create a tight coupling), it fires an event. Any listener can subscribe to react to the status change. This is the **Observer pattern**.

### The `?.Invoke()` syntax
`OnStatusChanged?.Invoke(msg)` — the `?.` means "only invoke if OnStatusChanged is not null (i.e., someone has subscribed)". Without `?.`, invoking a null delegate throws a `NullReferenceException`.

### Why NOT just call NotificationService directly
```csharp
// BAD — Application is now coupled to NotificationService
NotificationService.Send(this._jobSeekerId, ...);
```
`Application` would need to import and know about `NotificationService`. With an event, `Application` only knows "something happened" and it's the caller's job to wire up the notification. Loose coupling is better for maintenance.

---

## 23. RECORD – OTPRecord

### File: `Features/Auth/OTPService.cs`

```csharp
private record OTPRecord(string Code, DateTime ExpiresAt, int Attempts);
```

### Usage
```csharp
_otpStore[key] = record with { Attempts = record.Attempts + 1 };
```

### What a record is
A `record` is a special class (C# 9+) that is:
- **Immutable by default** — properties are init-only
- **Value-equality** — two OTPRecords with the same Code/ExpiresAt/Attempts are considered equal
- **With-expression support** — `record with { Attempts = x }` creates a new copy with just that field changed

### Why record for OTPRecord
OTP data should be immutable. Once generated, the Code and ExpiresAt never change — only Attempts increments. Instead of mutating a field, the code creates a brand-new `OTPRecord` with the incremented Attempts. This prevents accidental mutation of OTP data.

```csharp
// Instead of: record.Attempts++ (mutates)
_otpStore[key] = record with { Attempts = record.Attempts + 1 }; // creates a new record
```

### Why NOT a regular class
A regular class would allow `record.ExpiresAt = DateTime.Now` anywhere, which could be used to extend OTP validity — a security vulnerability. The `record` pattern enforces immutability naturally.

---

## 24. METHOD OVERLOADING – JobSearch.SearchJobs

### File: `Features/JobPosting/JobSearch.cs`

```csharp
// Overload 1: No filters — return all active jobs
public static List<JobListing> SearchJobs(List<JobListing> jobs) { ... }

// Overload 2: Filter by keyword
public static List<JobListing> SearchJobs(List<JobListing> jobs, string keyword) { ... }

// Overload 3: Filter by keyword AND salary range
public static List<JobListing> SearchJobs(List<JobListing> jobs, string keyword, SalaryRange range) { ... }
```

### What method overloading is
Multiple methods with the **same name** but **different parameter lists**. The compiler picks the correct one based on the arguments you pass.

### Why overloading is used here
Job search has multiple modes depending on what the user provides:
- Browse all jobs → call with just `jobs`
- Search by keyword → call with `jobs, keyword`
- Search with salary filter → call with `jobs, keyword, range`

Without overloading, you'd need `SearchAllJobs()`, `SearchJobsByKeyword()`, `SearchJobsByKeywordAndSalary()` — ugly names. Overloading makes the API cleaner.

### Chain reuse (overloads calling each other)
```csharp
public static List<JobListing> SearchJobs(List<JobListing> jobs, string keyword, SalaryRange range)
{
    return SearchJobs(jobs, keyword)    // ← reuses the 2-param overload
        .Where(j => j.SalaryRange.Min >= range.Min && j.SalaryRange.Max <= range.Max)
        .ToList();
}
```
The 3-param overload calls the 2-param overload, then adds salary filtering. No code duplication.

---

## 25. SWITCH EXPRESSION AND PATTERN MATCHING

### Where it appears

```csharp
// Application.cs — state machine transition validation
bool valid = (_status, parsed) switch
{
    (ApplicationStatus.PENDING,    ApplicationStatus.SHORTLISTED)         => true,
    (ApplicationStatus.PENDING,    ApplicationStatus.REJECTED)            => true,
    (ApplicationStatus.SHORTLISTED,ApplicationStatus.INTERVIEW_SCHEDULED) => true,
    (ApplicationStatus.SHORTLISTED,ApplicationStatus.REJECTED)            => true,
    (ApplicationStatus.SHORTLISTED,ApplicationStatus.HIRED)               => true,
    _                                                                      => false
};

// Application.cs — status badge display
public string GetStatusBadge() => _status switch
{
    ApplicationStatus.PENDING             => "[PENDING]",
    ApplicationStatus.SHORTLISTED         => "[SHORTLISTED]",
    ApplicationStatus.INTERVIEW_SCHEDULED => "[INTERVIEW SCHEDULED]",
    ApplicationStatus.REJECTED            => "[REJECTED]",
    ApplicationStatus.HIRED               => "[HIRED]",
    _                                     => $"[{_status}]"
};
```

### What switch expression is
A C# 8+ feature — an expression (produces a value) rather than a statement (like old `switch { case: break; }`). The `_` is the discard pattern — it matches anything (like `default:`).

### Why tuple pattern `(_status, parsed) switch`
You're matching on a PAIR of values simultaneously. Without tuple pattern matching, you'd need nested ifs:
```csharp
// OLD WAY — nested ifs, verbose, error-prone
if (_status == PENDING && parsed == SHORTLISTED) valid = true;
else if (_status == PENDING && parsed == REJECTED) valid = true;
else if (_status == SHORTLISTED && parsed == INTERVIEW_SCHEDULED) valid = true;
// ... 4 more else-ifs ...
else valid = false;
```
The switch expression is a visual table of valid transitions — far easier to see at a glance which transitions are allowed.

### Why this is the State Machine pattern
`UpdateStatus()` enforces that status can only flow in valid directions:
- `PENDING → SHORTLISTED` ✓
- `PENDING → HIRED` ✗ (skipping stages is blocked)
- `REJECTED → HIRED` ✗ (can't reverse a rejection)

This protects business logic integrity without scattered `if` checks everywhere.

---

## 26. COPY CONSTRUCTOR – Application

### File: `Features/Application/Application.cs`

```csharp
// Copy constructor
public Application(Application existing)
{
    _applicationId  = Guid.NewGuid().ToString(); // NEW ID for the copy
    _jobSeekerId    = existing._jobSeekerId;
    _jobId          = existing._jobId;
    _appliedDate    = existing._appliedDate;
    _status         = existing._status;
    _resumeSnapshot = existing._resumeSnapshot;
    _coverLetter    = existing._coverLetter;
}
```

### What a copy constructor is
A constructor that takes an existing object of the same type and creates a new object with the same data.

### Why used here
When you need to create a duplicate application (e.g., for archiving or audit trails), you pass the existing one to this constructor. Notice it generates a NEW `_applicationId` — the copy is a distinct entity, not an alias.

### Why NOT just `=` assignment
In C#, assigning objects copies the reference, not the object:
```csharp
Application copy = original; // BOTH variables point to the SAME object
copy._status = "HIRED";      // also changes original!
```
The copy constructor creates a genuinely independent new object.

---

## 27. STATIC COUNTER FIELD – JobSeeker._counter

### File: `Features/Candidate/JobSeeker.cs`

```csharp
private static int _counter = 1;

private static string GenerateSeekerId()
{
    string id = $"JSK-{DateTime.Now.Year}-{_counter:D4}";
    _counter++;
    return id;
}
```

### What static field means on an instance class
A `static` field on an instance class belongs to the CLASS, not to any individual object. All `JobSeeker` instances share the same `_counter` value.

### Why static counter
To generate sequential IDs like `JSK-2026-0001`, `JSK-2026-0002`, etc., you need a counter that persists across all seeker objects. If `_counter` were an instance field, every new seeker would start with `_counter = 1` and all seekers would get ID `JSK-2026-0001`.

### Why NOT use a database auto-increment
This is a console application with JSON file storage, not a database. The static counter is a simple in-memory solution. Note: the counter resets on app restart (it's not persisted), but the `SeekerId` IS saved to JSON, so old IDs are preserved.

---

## 28. NULLABLE REFERENCE TYPES AND NULL-CONDITIONAL OPERATOR

### Where it appears

```csharp
// Nullable type — variable CAN be null
Admin? admin   = null;
JobSeeker? seeker = null;

// Null-conditional operator — only call if not null
string identifier = Console.ReadLine()?.Trim() ?? "";

// Null-forgiving operator — "I know this isn't null"
private static FileStorageService<JobSeeker> _seekerStorage = null!;

// Null-coalescing — return default if null
return JobSeekers.FirstOrDefault(s => s.Email == id)?.Name ?? "Unknown";
```

### What `?` after type means
`Admin?` means "this variable can hold either an `Admin` object or `null`". Without `?`, C# (with nullable reference types enabled) treats `Admin admin` as guaranteed non-null and warns if you assign null to it.

### Why `Admin? admin = null`
In HandleLogin, we don't know yet if the user is an admin or a seeker. Both start as `null` and one gets assigned after the lookup. The `?` tells both the compiler and the reader: "this might not be filled in".

### Why `Console.ReadLine()?.Trim() ?? ""`
`Console.ReadLine()` can return `null` (e.g., at end-of-stream). Without `?.`, calling `.Trim()` on null throws `NullReferenceException`. The `?.` means "call Trim() only if not null; otherwise return null". The `?? ""` means "if the result is null, use empty string instead".

### Why `null!` for DataStore fields
```csharp
private static FileStorageService<JobSeeker> _seekerStorage = null!;
```
The field starts as null but gets assigned in `LoadAll()` before any other code uses it. The `null!` tells the compiler "I know this is null now, but I guarantee it won't be null when used — stop warning me."

---

## 29. ASYNC / AWAIT – SendNotificationAsync

### File: `Features/Notifications/NotificationService.cs`

```csharp
public async Task SendNotificationAsync(string userId, string title, string message, string type = "ANNOUNCEMENT")
{
    await Task.Run(() =>
    {
        var n = new Notification(userId, title, message, type);
        _notifications.Add(n);
        Save();
    });
}
```

### What async/await is
`async` marks a method as asynchronous — it can `await` other async operations without blocking the calling thread. `Task.Run()` moves work to a background thread.

### Why async notification
Saving to disk (writing a JSON file) is I/O-bound work. In a real production system, you'd want this to be non-blocking so the UI remains responsive while the notification is being saved. The `async` version demonstrates the pattern even in this console app.

### Why NOT make EVERYTHING async
Most methods in this app are synchronous because it's a simple console app. Adding `async` everywhere when it's not needed introduces complexity (you'd need `await` everywhere you call them) without benefit. It's used here specifically to show the async pattern for the one I/O-heavy operation that would benefit from it.

---

## 30. DICTIONARY / HASHSET COLLECTIONS

### Where they appear

```csharp
// OTPService.cs — fast lookup by email key
private readonly Dictionary<string, OTPRecord> _otpStore = new();

// AuthService.cs — track lock expiry times
private static readonly Dictionary<string, DateTime> _lockTimestamps = new();

// ResumeService.cs — O(1) extension check
private static readonly HashSet<string> _allowedExtensions = new(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
{ ".pdf", ".doc", ".docx" };

// DataStore.cs — fast seeker lookup by email
public static Dictionary<string, JobSeeker> JobSeekerIndex { get; private set; } = new();
```

### Why Dictionary for OTP store
OTPs are looked up by email key: `_otpStore[seeker.Email]`. Dictionary gives O(1) average-case lookup — instant, regardless of how many OTPs are in flight. Using a `List<OTPEntry>` and scanning by email would be O(n).

### Why HashSet for allowed extensions
```csharp
if (!_allowedExtensions.Contains(ext)) throw ...
```
`HashSet.Contains()` is O(1). `List<string>.Contains()` is O(n). For a 3-item list it barely matters, but `HashSet` is semantically correct for "a set of allowed values" — it also automatically prevents duplicates.

### `StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase`
This makes the HashSet case-insensitive: `.PDF`, `.Pdf`, `.pdf` all match `.pdf`. Without this, `.PDF` uploads would be rejected even though they're valid PDF files.

### Why JobSeekerIndex (Dictionary)
```csharp
// Fast path — O(1)
if (JobSeekerIndex.TryGetValue(email, out var seeker)) return seeker;
```
`DataStore.FindJobSeeker()` is called on EVERY login. With 10,000 users, a linear scan would be slow. The dictionary index makes it instant. `RebuildIndex()` is called whenever seekers are added/modified to keep the index in sync.

---

## 31. GUID.NEWGUID() FOR UNIQUE IDs

### Where it appears

```csharp
// JobListing.cs
_jobId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();

// Application.cs
_applicationId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();

// Notification.cs
_notifId = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();
```

### What a GUID is
A **Globally Unique Identifier** — a 128-bit number generated to be statistically unique across all computers, all time. Format: `"3f2504e0-4f89-11d3-9a0c-0305e82c3301"`.

### Why GUIDs for job/application/notification IDs
These objects are created by any user at any time and saved to JSON files. There's no central database to auto-increment IDs. GUIDs ensure no two jobs or applications ever accidentally get the same ID, even if the app is restarted or data is manually edited.

### Why NOT sequential numbers (1, 2, 3...)
Sequential numbers require a synchronized counter. Across multiple sessions (app restarts), you'd have to persist the last-used number. You'd also risk collisions if two users created applications simultaneously (in a multi-user scenario). GUIDs avoid all of this.

### Note: JobSeeker uses a different pattern
`JSK-2026-0001` — a human-readable sequential ID. This is intentional: job seekers log in with their ID and need to remember/type it. `3f2504e0-...` would be unusable as a login identifier.

---

## 32. BCRYPT PASSWORD HASHING

### File: `Features/Auth/AuthService.cs`

```csharp
public static string HashPassword(string rawPassword)
{
    return BCrypt.Net.BCrypt.HashPassword(rawPassword, workFactor: 11);
}

public static bool VerifyPassword(string rawPassword, string hash)
{
    return BCrypt.Net.BCrypt.Verify(rawPassword, hash);
}
```

### What BCrypt is
BCrypt is a password hashing function designed specifically for passwords. It:
1. Is intentionally SLOW (controlled by `workFactor`) — makes brute-force attacks costly
2. Automatically generates and stores a random SALT — prevents rainbow table attacks
3. Is one-way — you cannot reverse a hash to get the original password

### Why `workFactor: 11`
`workFactor` is the number of BCrypt rounds (2^11 = 2048 iterations). Higher = slower = harder to crack. Factor 11 takes ~100ms to compute — negligible for a user logging in, but makes brute-forcing 1 billion passwords take centuries.

### Why NOT MD5 or SHA256
MD5/SHA256 are general-purpose hash functions — fast (by design). An attacker with a GPU can compute billions of SHA256 hashes per second. BCrypt is specifically engineered to be slow and resist GPU attacks.

### Why NOT store passwords in plain text
If the JSON data file is ever exposed, plain-text passwords would immediately compromise all users. With BCrypt, the hashes are useless without the original passwords.

---

## 33. SYSTEM.TEXT.JSON SERIALIZATION

### File: `FileStorage/FileStorageService.cs`

```csharp
private static readonly JsonSerializerOptions _opts = new()
{
    WriteIndented = true,
    PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true,
    Converters = { new System.Text.Json.Serialization.JsonStringEnumConverter() }
};

public void SaveData(List<T> data)
{
    string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(data, _opts);
    File.WriteAllText(_filePath, json);
}

public List<T> LoadData()
{
    string json = File.ReadAllText(_filePath);
    return JsonSerializer.Deserialize<List<T>>(json, _opts) ?? new List<T>();
}
```

### Why JSON for persistence
JSON is human-readable, editable with any text editor, and natively supported by .NET without external dependencies. For a console app prototype, JSON files are simpler than setting up a database.

### Why `WriteIndented = true`
Produces formatted JSON with line breaks and indentation, making the data files readable for debugging:
```json
{
  "Name": "Alice",
  "Email": "alice@example.com"
}
```
vs minified: `{"Name":"Alice","Email":"alice@example.com"}`

### Why `PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true`
When deserializing, if the JSON has `"name"` but the C# property is `Name`, case-insensitive matching handles it. This makes the storage resilient to minor JSON edits.

### Why `JsonStringEnumConverter`
Without this, enums serialize as numbers (`0`, `1`, `2`). With it, they serialize as strings (`"PENDING"`, `"APPROVED"`). Human-readable files are much easier to debug: `"Status": "SHORTLISTED"` vs `"Status": 1`.

---

## 34. SEALED OVERRIDE – GetHashCode in JobListing

### File: `Features/JobPosting/JobListing.cs`

```csharp
public sealed override int GetHashCode() => _jobId.GetHashCode();
```

### What this means
`sealed override` — overrides the base class method (`object.GetHashCode`) AND prevents any subclass from overriding it again.

### Why override GetHashCode
When you put `JobListing` objects into a `HashSet` or use them as `Dictionary` keys, .NET needs a hash code to determine the bucket. The default `GetHashCode()` is based on object identity (memory address) — two different `JobListing` objects for the same job would have different hash codes. By basing it on `_jobId`, two objects representing the same job get the same hash code.

### Why `sealed`
Once you've defined that `JobListing` equality is based on `_jobId`, no subclass should be able to change that rule. `sealed` locks it down.

---

## 35. DESTRUCTOR / FINALIZER – ~JobListing

### File: `Features/JobPosting/JobListing.cs`

```csharp
~JobListing() { }
```

### What a destructor is
`~ClassName()` is a **finalizer** (destructor). It is called by the garbage collector before the object's memory is reclaimed. You'd use it to release unmanaged resources (file handles, native memory, network connections).

### Why it's here (and why it's empty)
The empty destructor in this project is **demonstrative** — it shows you know the finalizer syntax exists. In real code:
- An empty finalizer actually HURTS performance (it forces the GC to run the finalization queue)
- You should only add a finalizer if you hold unmanaged resources
- The proper pattern is to implement `IDisposable` and call `GC.SuppressFinalize(this)` in `Dispose()`

In this project, `JobListing` holds no unmanaged resources (just C# strings and lists), so the finalizer does nothing meaningful. Its purpose here is educational.

---

## 36. VIRTUAL METHOD ExportToFile IN JobListing

### File: `Features/JobPosting/JobListing.cs`

```csharp
public virtual void ExportToFile(string path)
{
    File.WriteAllText(path, ToString());
}
```

### Why virtual
This export logic writes a simple string representation. A subclass like `PremiumJobListing` could override this to export a formatted PDF or HTML file instead, while still using the same method signature. The `virtual` keyword opens the door for polymorphic export behavior.

### Why NOT abstract
The base implementation (writing `ToString()` to a text file) is a valid default. Not every subclass needs to override it. `virtual` provides the default while allowing override; `abstract` forces every subclass to implement from scratch.

---
## 37. REPOSITORY / DATASTORE PATTERN

### File: `FileStorage/DataStore.cs`

```csharp
internal static class DataStore
{
    public static List<JobSeeker>   JobSeekers   { get; private set; } = new();
    public static List<Admin>       Admins       { get; private set; } = new();
    public static List<JobListing>  Jobs         { get; private set; } = new();
    public static List<Application> Applications { get; private set; } = new();

    public static void LoadAll() { ... }
    public static void SaveJobSeekers() { ... }
    public static JobSeeker? FindJobSeeker(string identifier) { ... }
}
```

### What Repository Pattern is
A class that centralizes all data access operations. All code that needs to read or write data goes through the repository, not directly to files or databases.

### Why DataStore is the central repository
Instead of every class knowing about JSON files and paths, they all call `DataStore.FindJobSeeker()`, `DataStore.Jobs`, `DataStore.SaveApplications()`. If you ever replace JSON files with a real database, you only change `DataStore` — nothing else in the application needs to change. The rest of the code stays identical.

### Why `{ get; private set; }` on the lists
External code can READ the lists (`DataStore.Jobs`) but cannot REPLACE them (`DataStore.Jobs = newList` is rejected by the compiler). This prevents a bug where someone accidentally clears the entire job list. To modify the list, you call `DataStore.Jobs.Add(job)` and `DataStore.SaveJobs()` — controlled operations.

---

## 38. INDEX DICTIONARY – JobSeekerIndex

### File: `FileStorage/DataStore.cs`

```csharp
public static Dictionary<string, JobSeeker> JobSeekerIndex { get; private set; } = new();

private static void RebuildIndex()
{
    JobSeekerIndex = JobSeekers.ToDictionary(
        s => s.Email.ToLowerInvariant(),
        s => s,
        StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
}
```

### What this is
An in-memory index — a secondary data structure that provides O(1) lookups by a specific key (email), complementing the primary `List<JobSeeker>`.

### Why maintain a separate index
`DataStore.FindJobSeeker()` is called on every login. With 10,000 users in the list, a linear scan `JobSeekers.FirstOrDefault(s => s.Email == email)` checks up to 10,000 records. The dictionary index checks exactly 1 bucket — constant time regardless of list size.

### Why rebuild after every save
```csharp
public static void SaveJobSeekers()
{
    _seekerStorage.SaveData(JobSeekers);
    RebuildIndex(); // ← keeps index in sync
}
```
When a new seeker is added or email is changed, the index must reflect the new state. Rebuilding ensures the index never becomes stale.

### Why `StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase`
Login with `"ALICE@GMAIL.COM"` should find `"alice@gmail.com"`. The comparer makes the dictionary key lookup case-insensitive.

